00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.680
So the first question from
YouTube, Vivek the Sai

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.560
how do we level the playing
field in low funded schools?

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.270
It's a good question, you know,

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.473
I think arguably Khan Academy with the

00:02:25.473 --> 00:02:29.240
mission of free world-class
education for anyone anywhere

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.500
it's implicit that we are trying
to level the playing field,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.870
especially for folks who are low income,

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:38.110
or might be attending
schools with less resources,

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.433
or maybe don't even
have access to schools.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.550
And so one theory of
action for us is that,

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:46.980
you know, hopefully folks will be able to

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.090
leverage things like Khan Academy

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:50.670
and start as students
using it on their own,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.810
but you know, half of our usages teachers

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:54.930
using Khan Academy as
part of their classrooms,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.040
where students are able to
work on Khan Academy exercises,

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.430
sometimes aligned to the
student teacher's lessons.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:01.970
Sometimes the teachers
,students are doing it their

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.000
own time and pace.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.010
And teachers are able to then understand

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.450
where all of their kids are and

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.163
then support them more
inside of the classroom.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:11.530
We've seen a lot of efficacy studies

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.080
that can make a huge difference.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.130
Now, clearly, in order to use Khan Academy

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.920
that way there's some prerequisite things

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.380
that have to exist internet connections ,

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.570
for sure at the school
devices at the school,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.130
ideally it would be at
the home and school.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.040
we're seeing that a lot with COVID.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.170
But you know this is
one area that we think

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.580
we can help level start help
leveling the playing field.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.690
I don't wanna pretend like
it's a simple solution

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:34.250
and that you can just snap your fingers

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.620
and that everything is perfect.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.950
Some of y'all know, I have
another skunk work project.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:40.556
It is not a part of Khan Academy ,

00:03:40.556 --> 00:03:43.210
it's actually incorporating
it as we speak as a

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:46.200
separate non-for-profit
called schoolhouse.world

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.100
to try to get more folks
access to tutoring as well.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.300
So that, you know, not only
can you get all the practice,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.210
the feedback, the video supports
the instructional supports,

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.130
the teacher supports through Khan Academy,

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.960
but you can also become, you
know, connect to other people

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.620
and really become part of
a community of learning

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:05.240
is really my dream.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.550
Because in both of those cases,

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.940
learning is not bound by time or space,

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.680
learning can meet you where you are

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.350
and then, you know the
zip code that you live in

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:15.810
or the income of your family

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.700
or the neighborhoods you
live in should matter less.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.710
Unfortunately it always
probably play a factor,

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:23.250
but it will hopefully matter a lot less.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:28.250
See, there's a question
see Jim Sugar Brown

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.750
from Facebook asks ,

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:31.790
Sal, how do you see the future
of education and learning?

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:33.830
Can you differentiate between the two?

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.920
So that's a big question,
I like that you're trying,

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:36.753
you know, you're saying, well,

00:04:36.753 --> 00:04:38.860
the different education and learning,

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.160
you know when I at
least imagine education,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.620
I imagined all of the things
that the education system does,

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:46.420
there's a learning component of it

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:48.180
and we're traditionally thinking of like

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.660
core academic skills then
there is a socialization,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.630
you know, a lot of us, we
learned to kinda of work

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.770
with others in many years of school.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:58.510
And then another thing
that the school system does

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.523
is kinda of a credentialing aspect to it.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.530
I think we're learning with
COVID also the school system,

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:07.240
especially for younger kids
provides a childcare function,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.210
but it's, it's really
academics, credentialing,

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:14.090
socialization and kinda of care of kids.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:15.980
And there's other supports
that the education system

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.840
does as well things like lunch programs

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.310
and other social supports.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.320
You know, the learning you
could think its broader

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:24.950
it's a lot of that,

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.900
but you could, you know what
when I imagine learning,

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.190
I imagine, you know,

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:31.190
me using YouTube to figure
out how to fix my toilet,

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:36.190
which I've done several times
especially during COVID or,

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.560
you know, just learning
how to play guitar.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.360
And arguably some of these
things could be part of

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:42.120
the education system as well.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.260
But I think in either case,

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.350
we're gonna be moving
to a world where there's

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:50.400
gonna be far more paths
that people can take,

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.320
and they're gonna be far more customizable

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.670
to the needs of the individual.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:58.030
And as these, as the
paths that you can take,

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:01.670
become more personalized and more diverse,

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:03.840
then there's gonna be more universal ways

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.080
of proving what you know,
creating a signal to you know,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.310
you could call it a credential
so that other people

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:12.560
can understand what you are capable of.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.630
I also think you're
going to see, you know,

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:16.720
I've I talked about the
schoolhouse.world project,

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:18.840
which is separate from Khan Academy,

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:21.180
but I also think you're
gonna see more mechanisms

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.600
where communities of people
are going to be able to

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.500
support each other and kind
of a very grassroots way

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.170
which I think could be a
very powerful, you know,

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.570
I think historically the last 200 years,

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:34.890
education and learning has
been a very centralized affair.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:35.920
It's it's been, you know,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.160
a couple of folks get
together in a room, I mean,

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.040
famously the courses that
you, many of you all learned,

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.810
or we all learned in high
school were determined

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.440
by 10 university presidents in 1890

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:48.810
and then there's the community
of 10 you can look it up.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.220
And so that's obviously
a centralized as it gets.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.250
So I think you're gonna start
seeing a lot more diversity

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:57.290
and paths but you're gonna
start seeing much stronger ways

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.090
that you can prove what you know

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:01.890
and really community being a
very powerful way to learn.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:07.407
So there's from YouTube,
Smart Bear asking,

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:10.210
Sal, can you tell me
how did you make it to

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:13.760
where you are right now,
founder and CEO of Khan Academy.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.223
I oftentimes wonder that myself.

00:07:19.019 --> 00:07:21.560
Oh you know, I think growing up,

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.390
I think we all have this kinda angst

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.480
and frankly, the angst
hasn't gone away yet

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:27.100
that you know, we have one life,

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.100
how do we make it count

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.870
or how do we make it worth something?

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:33.150
And you know, we can debate
what it means to count

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.910
or what it means to want something.

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:37.650
I believe that I've
evolved over over time,

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:41.790
you know, when I was a teenager
or in my early twenties,

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:45.940
I think, you know, I imagined counting,

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:47.130
making a dent in the universe,

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:50.443
being all about kind of external,

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.750
whatever impact external, you know,

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:58.750
whether it's discover a new
drug that could cure a disease

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.693
or impacting people in a
certain way, or, you know,

00:08:03.693 --> 00:08:06.730
I'd have to admit especially
early in probably even now,

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:10.230
but early in my life, I probably
was motivated to saying,

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:12.680
hey, I would like to be able to, you know,

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.710
I grew up not so wealthy

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:18.900
and back you know, my mom I
think made $16,000 in 1994,

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.040
which was a year that I had
to help her fill out the taxes

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:22.370
to get financial aid for college.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.580
So we didn't have a
lot of money, you know,

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.720
she was making roughly minimum wage

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.500
so there was in me, a lot of,

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:30.340
I would say a fire in my belly

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:34.260
to try to be financially comfortable

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:36.030
and not have to kinda
go through some of the

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.380
financial uncertainties
that we had growing up.

00:08:39.474 --> 00:08:42.240
And anyway , so I did what I
thought would make sense there,

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.633
but I never like was
just one of these kids

00:08:43.633 --> 00:08:44.850
that it was just like, okay,

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.280
what do I need to do to get a job?

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:47.740
You know, what's gonna be on the test?

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.200
Let me just ace it, I naturally, I mean,

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.790
I think y'all can tell
my personality no matter

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:57.080
how pragmatic I try to be my,
I do a lot of daydreaming.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.630
My personality tends to wander into the

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:04.530
I don't know what you would
call it, the aspirational,

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:09.530
the delusional, the imagining
what the world could be.

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:13.380
And I've often felt this
angst that like, you know,

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.620
we only have so many
decades on this planet

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:16.910
let's make it interesting.

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:18.350
You know, let's not just try to,

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.150
let's just not try to
go through the mold that

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:20.983
everyone else has.

00:09:20.983 --> 00:09:22.380
There's certain things
we have to do to be able

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:23.850
to pay the bills and you know,

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:25.890
hopefully get a house and pay
off your debts, et cetera,

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:27.650
et cetera, support your family.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:31.650
But, you know, we got let's
make it interesting too.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:36.597
So anyway, you know, college
I tried to do my best that,

00:09:36.597 --> 00:09:39.400
you know, to kinda get a
good job at had a good job,

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:44.070
you know ,in 1998 came out
here to Silicon Valley,

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:46.270
worked at Oracle as a product manager.

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:48.020
It was a good job had a really good boss

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:48.980
and he was Thomas Kurian.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:50.920
He's actually quite
known in tech circles now

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:52.120
he runs all of Google Cloud,

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.380
but I was one of the first
people he ever hired.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:57.000
He was in his early thirties at the time.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:01.290
And then, you know, it was
startup, it was the.com mania.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:04.330
I went joined a startup
that was a great experience.

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.150
But then, you know, the
NASDAQ started to tank

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:07.920
and I said, actually I
should take some shelter

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:09.240
in business school and
try to figure out what

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.020
I wanna do with my life.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.840
I also say, you know, I
went to business school

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:17.140
because I was lonely and I had
this theory that in Boston,

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:18.050
there were a lot more students

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:20.803
and I could probably find,
you know, a life partner.

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:24.700
So I went back to Boston
for this goes true

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:26.178
both things happen i
figured out what I at least

00:10:26.178 --> 00:10:29.190
what I wanna do career
wise for my next stage.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:33.117
And I met my now actually
I met my wife back in 1997

00:10:33.117 --> 00:10:36.380
and you know, I had always aspired

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:38.320
to get to know her better for many years.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.694
And but then in 2002, I got,

00:10:40.694 --> 00:10:45.694
I was successful and she
hasn't figured it out just yet

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.500
that, you know, I've
definitely been the winner

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.420
on this situation.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:55.900
But the anyway, I'm probably
going into more of my biography

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:58.660
than you're asking, but,
you know it was kind of,

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:02.150
it's been like this kind
of a very securest path,

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:04.470
but it was I think many of y'all
know, you could find videos

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.300
on YouTube and other places of me telling

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.790
the Khan Academy Genesis
story, but it was really 2004.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.880
I was a analyst at a hedge
fund cousin needed help

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:13.090
and but in the back of my mind,

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:14.326
I had always thought about that

00:11:14.326 --> 00:11:16.480
you know, maybe technology
could be a really powerful tool

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:18.640
to scalable help a lot of folks.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:21.780
And so that's when I
started tutoring cousin's

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.980
word spread that free
tutoring was going on.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:25.830
Then I started tutoring more cousins.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:28.410
I started making software
for them, videos for them,

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.150
people who are not my
cousins like yourselves,

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.680
we're starting to use them by 2009.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:37.110
I had trouble focusing on my
day job, 2000 so I quit it.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:39.470
We were living off of
savings that was scary

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:41.590
I mean, you know, you're
asking, how do I get here?

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:44.200
You know yeah, you're
gonna have these moments

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.250
of having to kinda jump into scary things.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:48.790
And you're gonna question yourself

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:50.360
and you're gonna, you know that year

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:51.800
that Khan Academy had no funding

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:52.880
and I was living off of savings ,

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:54.100
